Anna —

Ten turns of the glass. We held eleven, because trust the Home Companies to be late to their own rescue.

Lady Harrow stood in her scorched doorway, thanked us with great formality, then took down her grandfather's boar-spear from above the hearth and informed us she was coming along. I have stopped arguing with the gentry. They feed us.

Everything now runs down to one road. The King's Road, the old one, straight through the pine country to the gates of Castle Corven, where the Winter Duke sits rehearsing his coronation. He knows we are coming — we are past the season of surprises. He has put his best on the road to break us before we ever see the walls: knights who knelt for pay, pikemen from the border companies, and slingers from the hills who can put a stone through a helmet at forty paces.

Aldric drilled us on the pines until dark. Keep to the trees, watch the pairs — where one of the Duke's men stands, his partner is circling to your far side. They fight like mirrors of us because that is exactly what they are.

The difference, Wren says, is that mirrors fight for silver.

We fight for the two coppers at the tollgate, for Corben who snored, for Harrowfield's blackened beams, and for a boy-king hidden somewhere in the hills whose name I will not put in a letter even to you.

One road left, sister.

— Elin
